1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,138
In April of 1861, the United States went to war with itself.

2
00:00:04,268 --> 00:00:06,652
Over 600,000 Americans would die.

3
00:00:06,782 --> 00:00:07,990
Here is the strange part.

4
00:00:08,120 --> 00:00:11,046
Roughly three out of four white southerners did not own a single

5
00:00:11,066 --> 00:00:12,053
enslaved person.

6
00:00:12,183 --> 00:00:15,304
So why would a region fight, and bleed, for a system most of its

7
00:00:15,324 --> 00:00:17,046
people had no direct stake in?

8
00:00:17,175 --> 00:00:20,491
To answer that, you have to go back seventy years, to a machine,

9
00:00:20,511 --> 00:00:23,523
a crop, and a prediction that turned out to be wrong.

10
00:00:23,653 --> 00:00:29,075
This is Chapter Sixteen: slavery in the United States, 1793 to 1860.

11
00:00:29,205 --> 00:00:32,585
In the 1780s, slavery looked like it might die on its own.

12
00:00:32,715 --> 00:00:35,491
Revolutionary idealism was in the air, and some southern

13
00:00:35,511 --> 00:00:39,355
leaders, Jefferson among them, talked openly about freeing their slaves.

14
00:00:39,485 --> 00:00:43,346
Others made a colder argument: economics would eventually expose

15
00:00:43,366 --> 00:00:46,223
slavery as unprofitable, and it would fade.

16
00:00:46,353 --> 00:00:47,771
The North was already moving.

17
00:00:47,901 --> 00:00:52,008
Vermont banned slavery in its constitution in 1777,

18
00:00:52,028 --> 00:00:56,702
Massachusetts in 1780, and Pennsylvania, New York, and New

19
00:00:56,722 --> 00:01:00,857
Jersey passed gradual emancipation laws by 1804.

20
00:01:00,987 --> 00:01:04,763
The Northwest Ordinance of 1787 kept slavery out of the Old

21
00:01:04,783 --> 00:01:06,256
Northwest entirely.

22
00:01:06,386 --> 00:01:10,432
Then, in 1793, Eli Whitney built a cotton gin.

23
00:01:10,562 --> 00:01:14,158
Before the gin, separating seeds from short-staple cotton was so

24
00:01:14,178 --> 00:01:16,179
slow that the crop barely paid.

25
00:01:16,309 --> 00:01:19,575
After it, one worker could clean fifty times as much in a day.

26
00:01:19,705 --> 00:01:22,793
Suddenly cotton was worth planting everywhere it would grow, and

27
00:01:22,813 --> 00:01:25,006
every new field needed labor to work it.

28
00:01:25,136 --> 00:01:26,319
The numbers tell the story.

29
00:01:26,449 --> 00:01:30,328
In 1790, the South produced about 1,000 tons of cotton a year.

30
00:01:30,458 --> 00:01:32,731
By 1860, it was 1,000,000 tons.

31
00:01:32,861 --> 00:01:36,357
In the same seventy years, the enslaved population grew from

32
00:01:36,377 --> 00:01:37,857
500,000 to 4,000,000.

33
00:01:37,987 --> 00:01:40,394
The prediction had been that slavery would wither.

34
00:01:40,524 --> 00:01:42,509
Instead, the gin gave it a second life.

35
00:01:42,639 --> 00:01:47,290
By the 1840s, southerners had a phrase for this: cotton is king.

36
00:01:47,420 --> 00:01:50,293
Look at what cotton meant to the national economy.

37
00:01:50,423 --> 00:01:53,984
In 1800, cotton was 7% of American exports.

38
00:01:54,114 --> 00:01:56,180
By 1820, 32%.

39
00:01:56,310 --> 00:02:01,814
By 1840, it crossed half, and by 1860 it was 57.5% of everything

40
00:02:01,834 --> 00:02:04,674
the United States sold abroad.

41
00:02:04,804 --> 00:02:07,959
The South grew more than half of the entire world's supply.

42
00:02:08,089 --> 00:02:10,300
That gave southern leaders a sense of leverage.

43
00:02:10,430 --> 00:02:13,700
Britain's textile mills ran on southern cotton, so southerners

44
00:02:13,720 --> 00:02:17,684
believed that if war ever came, Britain would have to take their side.

45
00:02:17,814 --> 00:02:19,812
But the money did not stay in the South.

46
00:02:19,942 --> 00:02:23,130
Northern shippers carried the cotton and took large profits.

47
00:02:23,260 --> 00:02:25,615
Northern bankers lent the planters money.

48
00:02:25,745 --> 00:02:27,627
New England mills spun the fiber.

49
00:02:27,757 --> 00:02:31,304
The prosperity of the North, the South, and England, as the

50
00:02:31,324 --> 00:02:35,021
textbook puts it, rested on the backs of enslaved people.

51
00:02:35,151 --> 00:02:38,542
Hold on to that, because it explains something later: why so

52
00:02:38,562 --> 00:02:42,621
many northerners, who disliked slavery, were in no hurry to end it.

53
00:02:42,751 --> 00:02:44,479
Put two maps side by side.

54
00:02:44,609 --> 00:02:48,429
In 1820, cotton production hugs the Atlantic coast: South

55
00:02:48,449 --> 00:02:51,327
Carolina, Georgia, a few river valleys.

56
00:02:51,457 --> 00:02:55,204
By 1860, a wide band of red runs from the Carolinas through

57
00:02:55,224 --> 00:02:58,857
Georgia and into Alabama, Mississippi, and Louisiana.

58
00:02:58,987 --> 00:03:02,543
Historians call this the black belt, named for the dark, fertile

59
00:03:02,563 --> 00:03:06,444
soil, and for the enslaved population that was forced to work it.

60
00:03:06,574 --> 00:03:09,850
In some of these counties, enslaved people were more than half

61
00:03:09,870 --> 00:03:10,918
the population.

62
00:03:11,048 --> 00:03:14,806
In Mississippi and South Carolina they were the majority of the entire state.

63
00:03:14,936 --> 00:03:17,107
Where did these 4,000,000 people come from?

64
00:03:17,237 --> 00:03:18,550
Not from Africa, mostly.

65
00:03:18,680 --> 00:03:22,618
Congress outlawed the legal importation of enslaved Africans in

66
00:03:22,638 --> 00:03:26,470
1808, a year after Britain abolished its own slave trade.

67
00:03:26,600 --> 00:03:29,279
Smuggling continued, but it was a small share.

68
00:03:29,409 --> 00:03:33,780
Most of the growth came from natural increase: children born into slavery.

69
00:03:33,910 --> 00:03:37,248
And because the Atlantic trade was closed, a vigorous internal

70
00:03:37,268 --> 00:03:39,306
slave trade grew up in its place.

71
00:03:39,436 --> 00:03:42,408
Enslaved people were sold from the older states of the upper

72
00:03:42,428 --> 00:03:45,320
South down to the new cotton lands of the Deep South.

73
00:03:45,450 --> 00:03:50,797
By 1860, planters held roughly $2,000,000,000 of capital in human beings.

74
00:03:50,927 --> 00:03:54,101
That was the single largest form of wealth in the region, larger

75
00:03:54,121 --> 00:03:55,419
than the land itself.

76
00:03:55,549 --> 00:03:59,167
Now the question from the beginning: who actually owned slaves?

77
00:03:59,297 --> 00:04:01,096
Fewer people than you might think.

78
00:04:01,226 --> 00:04:05,852
In 1850, only 1,733 families in the entire South owned 100 or

79
00:04:05,872 --> 00:04:07,722
more enslaved people.

80
00:04:07,852 --> 00:04:10,801
This tiny group was the planter aristocracy.

81
00:04:10,931 --> 00:04:13,853
They held the bulk of southern wealth and provided nearly all of

82
00:04:13,873 --> 00:04:15,778
its political and social leadership.

83
00:04:15,908 --> 00:04:19,155
Below them were tens of thousands of smaller slaveholders, most

84
00:04:19,175 --> 00:04:20,929
owning fewer than ten people.

85
00:04:21,059 --> 00:04:24,241
Add them all together and about one in four white southerners

86
00:04:24,261 --> 00:04:27,293
either owned slaves or belonged to a family that did.

87
00:04:27,423 --> 00:04:29,014
Which means three in four did not.

88
00:04:29,144 --> 00:04:32,151
Picture southern society in 1850 as a pyramid.

89
00:04:32,281 --> 00:04:35,780
At the very top, the slavocracy: the plantation owners.

90
00:04:35,910 --> 00:04:39,591
Below them, the plain folk: around 6,000,000 white yeoman

91
00:04:39,611 --> 00:04:42,199
farmers, most with no slaves at all.

92
00:04:42,329 --> 00:04:45,489
Below them, about 250,000 free Black people.

93
00:04:45,619 --> 00:04:49,228
At the base, 3,200,000 enslaved people.

94
00:04:49,358 --> 00:04:54,360
In total, the South held about 9,250,000 people, 40% of the

95
00:04:54,380 --> 00:04:56,333
national population.

96
00:04:56,463 --> 00:04:57,882
So back to the question.

97
00:04:58,012 --> 00:05:00,713
The plain folk had no direct economic stake in slavery.

98
00:05:00,843 --> 00:05:03,229
Yet they were among its stoutest defenders.

99
00:05:03,359 --> 00:05:03,925
Why?

100
00:05:04,055 --> 00:05:07,134
Partly hope: a poor farmer could imagine buying one enslaved

101
00:05:07,154 --> 00:05:10,606
person and rising, the southern version of the American dream.

102
00:05:10,736 --> 00:05:14,187
Partly fear: as long as 4,000,000 people were enslaved, even the

103
00:05:14,207 --> 00:05:16,627
poorest white man was not at the bottom.

104
00:05:16,757 --> 00:05:18,827
Slavery gave him a rung to stand on.

105
00:05:18,957 --> 00:05:21,930
Take that away, and some poorer whites would have been, as the

106
00:05:21,950 --> 00:05:24,373
textbook says, no better off than the slaves.

107
00:05:24,503 --> 00:05:27,278
The Cotton Kingdom looked mighty, but it had cracks.

108
00:05:27,408 --> 00:05:29,275
The economy was built on one crop.

109
00:05:29,405 --> 00:05:32,455
When world cotton prices fell, the whole region fell with them.

110
00:05:32,585 --> 00:05:36,255
Planters overspeculated in land and in people, borrowing against

111
00:05:36,275 --> 00:05:37,448
future harvests.

112
00:05:37,578 --> 00:05:40,557
An enslaved workforce was a heavy capital investment, and a

113
00:05:40,577 --> 00:05:44,029
single disease outbreak could wipe out an entire slave quarter.

114
00:05:44,159 --> 00:05:47,172
And the system kept something else out: immigrants.

115
00:05:47,302 --> 00:05:50,556
Europeans heading to America went North, where there were wages

116
00:05:50,576 --> 00:05:51,606
and cheap land.

117
00:05:51,736 --> 00:05:56,744
In 1860, only 4.4% of the southern population was foreign-born,

118
00:05:56,764 --> 00:05:59,169
against 18.7% in the North.

119
00:05:59,299 --> 00:06:02,438
Germans and Irish who did look South found themselves competing

120
00:06:02,458 --> 00:06:05,497
with slave labor, priced out of fertile land, and ignorant of

121
00:06:05,517 --> 00:06:06,519
cotton farming.

122
00:06:06,649 --> 00:06:08,937
So the North gained manpower and factories.

123
00:06:09,067 --> 00:06:11,137
The South gained more cotton fields.

124
00:06:11,267 --> 00:06:14,229
Southern planters watched the North grow rich off their crop and

125
00:06:14,249 --> 00:06:15,804
resented every dollar of it.

126
00:06:15,934 --> 00:06:17,976
Not every Black southerner was enslaved.

127
00:06:18,106 --> 00:06:22,571
By 1860 about 250,000 free Black people lived in the South.

128
00:06:22,701 --> 00:06:26,066
Some traced their freedom to the idealism of the Revolution,

129
00:06:26,086 --> 00:06:28,932
when some owners had emancipated their slaves.

130
00:06:29,062 --> 00:06:30,878
Some had purchased their own freedom.

131
00:06:31,008 --> 00:06:32,746
Many were of mixed ancestry.

132
00:06:32,876 --> 00:06:35,756
Some owned property, even, in a few cases, slaves.

133
00:06:35,886 --> 00:06:39,240
But they lived as what the textbook calls a third race, hemmed

134
00:06:39,260 --> 00:06:40,544
in by restrictions.

135
00:06:40,674 --> 00:06:42,444
And the North was not a refuge.

136
00:06:42,574 --> 00:06:46,143
Several northern states forbade free Black people from entering at all.

137
00:06:46,273 --> 00:06:47,929
Most denied them the vote.

138
00:06:48,059 --> 00:06:50,178
Some barred them from public schools.

139
00:06:50,308 --> 00:06:53,392
Irish immigrants, competing for the same low-wage jobs, were

140
00:06:53,412 --> 00:06:54,645
especially hostile.

141
00:06:54,775 --> 00:06:58,941
One observer put the contrast this way: southerners liked Black

142
00:06:58,961 --> 00:07:02,663
people as individuals but despised the race, while white

143
00:07:02,683 --> 00:07:07,983
northerners professed to like the race but disliked individual Black people.

144
00:07:08,113 --> 00:07:10,166
So how did enslaved people actually live?

145
00:07:10,296 --> 00:07:13,334
Conditions varied enormously, from the sugar parishes of

146
00:07:13,354 --> 00:07:15,624
Louisiana to a household in Virginia.

147
00:07:15,754 --> 00:07:17,384
But some things were constant.

148
00:07:17,514 --> 00:07:21,346
Enslaved people had no political rights and almost no legal protection.

149
00:07:21,476 --> 00:07:23,344
They could not testify in court.

150
00:07:23,474 --> 00:07:26,605
Their marriages were not legally recognized, which meant a

151
00:07:26,625 --> 00:07:29,863
family could be split at a sale with no legal recourse.

152
00:07:29,993 --> 00:07:31,264
Floggings were common.

153
00:07:31,394 --> 00:07:35,735
Enslaved people who resisted were sometimes sent to professional breakers.

154
00:07:35,865 --> 00:07:39,052
Even the economic logic was brutal: lash marks hurt resale

155
00:07:39,072 --> 00:07:42,480
value, so some owners restrained the whip not out of mercy but

156
00:07:42,500 --> 00:07:44,188
to protect the investment.

157
00:07:44,318 --> 00:07:45,907
The auction was the heart of the horror.

158
00:07:46,037 --> 00:07:49,165
Families were separated: a mother sold to Mississippi, her child

159
00:07:49,185 --> 00:07:50,271
kept in Virginia.

160
00:07:50,401 --> 00:07:53,930
Abolitionists called this slavery's greatest psychological

161
00:07:53,950 --> 00:07:57,479
cruelty, and Harriet Beecher Stowe built Uncle Tom's Cabin

162
00:07:57,499 --> 00:07:58,850
around it in 1852.

163
00:07:58,980 --> 00:07:59,786
And yet.

164
00:07:59,916 --> 00:08:03,273
Most enslaved people lived on larger plantations of twenty or

165
00:08:03,293 --> 00:08:06,090
more, where family life was relatively stable.

166
00:08:06,220 --> 00:08:08,546
Most were raised in two-parent households.

167
00:08:08,676 --> 00:08:12,000
A distinctive African American culture took shape: music, folk

168
00:08:12,020 --> 00:08:15,129
tales, and a Christianity reshaped during the Second Great

169
00:08:15,149 --> 00:08:17,718
Awakening into call and response preaching.

170
00:08:17,848 --> 00:08:20,107
Survival sometimes required a mask.

171
00:08:20,237 --> 00:08:23,802
Enslaved people learned to perform what white observers called

172
00:08:23,822 --> 00:08:27,503
the Sambo pattern, the grinning, harmless caricature that whites

173
00:08:27,523 --> 00:08:28,582
wanted to see.

174
00:08:28,712 --> 00:08:32,437
A former slave named John Little explained it better than any historian.

175
00:08:32,567 --> 00:08:36,188
They say slaves are happy, he wrote, because they laugh and are merry.

176
00:08:36,318 --> 00:08:40,150
He and three others had received 200 lashes in a day, with their

177
00:08:40,170 --> 00:08:41,383
feet in fetters.

178
00:08:41,513 --> 00:08:45,747
Yet at night, he said, we would sing and dance, and make others

179
00:08:45,767 --> 00:08:48,448
laugh at the rattling of our chains.

180
00:08:48,578 --> 00:08:51,252
We did it to keep down trouble, and to keep our hearts from

181
00:08:51,272 --> 00:08:52,618
being completely broken.

182
00:08:52,748 --> 00:08:54,603
Happy men we must have been, he wrote.

183
00:08:54,733 --> 00:08:57,622
Yet I have done it myself. I have cut capers in chains.

184
00:08:57,752 --> 00:09:01,554
When a textbook in 1932 could still call slavery the Negro's

185
00:09:01,574 --> 00:09:05,582
necessary transition to civilization, remember John Little.

186
00:09:05,712 --> 00:09:07,356
The conditions of slavery matter.

187
00:09:07,486 --> 00:09:09,439
The existence of slavery matters more.

188
00:09:09,569 --> 00:09:12,988
Enslaved people resisted every day, in ways that rarely made the

189
00:09:13,008 --> 00:09:14,010
history books.

190
00:09:14,140 --> 00:09:15,135
They ran away.

191
00:09:15,265 --> 00:09:16,795
They slowed the pace of work.

192
00:09:16,925 --> 00:09:19,803
They took food from the big house, broke tools, and occasionally

193
00:09:19,823 --> 00:09:21,205
poisoned a master's meal.

194
00:09:21,335 --> 00:09:22,721
And sometimes they rose up.

195
00:09:22,851 --> 00:09:26,522
In 1800, an enslaved man named Gabriel organized an armed

196
00:09:26,542 --> 00:09:29,059
insurrection in Richmond, Virginia.

197
00:09:29,189 --> 00:09:31,575
Informers betrayed it, and the leaders were hanged.

198
00:09:31,705 --> 00:09:37,062
In 1822, Denmark Vesey, a free Black man in Charleston, planned a rebellion.

199
00:09:37,192 --> 00:09:38,230
Informers again.

200
00:09:38,360 --> 00:09:41,332
Vesey and about thirty followers were publicly hanged.

201
00:09:41,462 --> 00:09:44,195
Then, in August of 1831, Nat Turner.

202
00:09:44,325 --> 00:09:47,294
A semiliterate preacher who claimed religious visions, Turner

203
00:09:47,314 --> 00:09:50,037
gathered around seventy followers in Southampton County,

204
00:09:50,057 --> 00:09:52,708
Virginia, and went from plantation to plantation.

205
00:09:52,838 --> 00:09:55,106
Around sixty white Virginians were killed.

206
00:09:55,236 --> 00:09:57,881
The uprising was crushed within days, and Turner and perhaps

207
00:09:57,901 --> 00:09:59,395
eighteen others were hanged.

208
00:09:59,525 --> 00:10:02,745
Eight years later, enslaved Africans seized the Spanish ship

209
00:10:02,765 --> 00:10:03,825
Amistad at sea.

210
00:10:03,955 --> 00:10:05,931
None of these rebellions could succeed.

211
00:10:06,061 --> 00:10:08,778
They lacked numbers, weapons, and organization.

212
00:10:08,908 --> 00:10:12,078
As one historian puts it, the only way a rebellion could have

213
00:10:12,098 --> 00:10:15,321
worked was if the government had aided the slaves, which would

214
00:10:15,341 --> 00:10:18,624
have meant the government, not the slaves, was in control.

215
00:10:18,754 --> 00:10:22,092
Every right enslaved people ever received came from those in

216
00:10:22,112 --> 00:10:25,674
power, and those in power made decisions based on profit, not on

217
00:10:25,694 --> 00:10:27,119
whether it was right.

218
00:10:27,249 --> 00:10:29,616
Turner's revolt failed as a rebellion.

219
00:10:29,746 --> 00:10:32,371
As a shock to the South, it succeeded completely.

220
00:10:32,501 --> 00:10:35,486
Opposition to slavery was as old as the Revolution.

221
00:10:35,616 --> 00:10:38,290
Quakers had condemned it in the 1770s.

222
00:10:38,420 --> 00:10:42,405
The first organized plan was not freedom in America but removal from it.

223
00:10:42,535 --> 00:10:46,584
In 1817 the American Colonization Society proposed sending Black

224
00:10:46,604 --> 00:10:48,444
Americans back to Africa.

225
00:10:48,574 --> 00:10:52,922
In 1822 it founded the Republic of Liberia on the West African coast.

226
00:10:53,052 --> 00:10:56,144
Over four decades about 15,000 people went.

227
00:10:56,274 --> 00:10:57,629
Most had no wish to go.

228
00:10:57,759 --> 00:11:01,407
By 1860 the great majority of enslaved people were native-born

229
00:11:01,427 --> 00:11:05,076
Americans with their own history and culture, and Africa was a

230
00:11:05,096 --> 00:11:06,411
foreign continent.

231
00:11:06,541 --> 00:11:08,421
The 1830s brought a harder edge.

232
00:11:08,551 --> 00:11:11,982
William Lloyd Garrison launched The Liberator, a newspaper that

233
00:11:12,002 --> 00:11:15,487
refused to tolerate what he called the poisonous weed of slavery

234
00:11:15,507 --> 00:11:18,112
and vowed to stamp it out, root and branch.

235
00:11:18,242 --> 00:11:21,294
In 1833 he and Wendell Phillips founded the American

236
00:11:21,314 --> 00:11:22,805
Anti-Slavery Society.

237
00:11:22,935 --> 00:11:25,159
Black abolitionists were already ahead of him.

238
00:11:25,289 --> 00:11:28,843
David Walker's Appeal to the Colored Citizens of the World, in

239
00:11:28,863 --> 00:11:31,938
1829, called for a bloody end to white supremacy.

240
00:11:32,068 --> 00:11:35,077
Sojourner Truth fought for emancipation and for women's rights

241
00:11:35,097 --> 00:11:36,178
at the same time.

242
00:11:36,308 --> 00:11:39,069
Martin Delany was one of the few Black leaders to take

243
00:11:39,089 --> 00:11:40,627
recolonization seriously.

244
00:11:40,757 --> 00:11:42,852
And then there was Frederick Douglass.

245
00:11:42,982 --> 00:11:45,975
Douglass escaped bondage in 1838 at twenty-one.

246
00:11:46,105 --> 00:11:50,177
In 1841 he gave an impromptu speech at an antislavery meeting in

247
00:11:50,197 --> 00:11:53,771
Massachusetts, and the abolitionists discovered him.

248
00:11:53,901 --> 00:11:57,524
He lectured despite beatings, and in 1845 published his

249
00:11:57,544 --> 00:12:01,569
Narrative, the classic American autobiography of slavery.

250
00:12:01,699 --> 00:12:05,470
Garrison burned a copy of the Constitution on the Fourth of

251
00:12:05,490 --> 00:12:10,366
July, 1854, calling it a covenant with death and an agreement with hell.

252
00:12:10,496 --> 00:12:13,263
Douglass took a different road: he looked to politics.

253
00:12:13,393 --> 00:12:17,718
He backed the Liberty Party in 1840, the Free Soil Party in

254
00:12:17,738 --> 00:12:21,228
1848, and the Republican Party in the 1850s.

255
00:12:21,358 --> 00:12:24,254
Knowledge, Douglass said, makes a man unfit to be a slave.

256
00:12:24,384 --> 00:12:28,059
Before 1830, some southerners still debated slavery, at least as

257
00:12:28,079 --> 00:12:29,310
a necessary evil.

258
00:12:29,440 --> 00:12:31,583
After 1830, the debate ended.

259
00:12:31,713 --> 00:12:32,750
What changed?

260
00:12:32,880 --> 00:12:34,536
Nat Turner, first of all.

261
00:12:34,666 --> 00:12:37,348
The revolt sent waves of hysteria through the South.

262
00:12:37,478 --> 00:12:38,978
Planters slept with pistols.

263
00:12:39,108 --> 00:12:42,675
Georgia offered $5,000 for Garrison's arrest, blaming him for

264
00:12:42,695 --> 00:12:43,886
inciting murder.

265
00:12:44,016 --> 00:12:47,662
Then the Nullification Crisis of 1832, when President Jackson

266
00:12:47,682 --> 00:12:51,509
denied South Carolina's right to nullify a federal law, deepened

267
00:12:51,529 --> 00:12:53,883
southern fears about federal power.

268
00:12:54,013 --> 00:12:55,801
Northern criticism stung.

269
00:12:55,931 --> 00:12:58,921
And southern preachers supplied an answer: the Bible never

270
00:12:58,941 --> 00:13:02,087
clearly condemned slavery, and religious leaders in scripture

271
00:13:02,107 --> 00:13:05,264
had owned slaves, so the institution must be acceptable.

272
00:13:05,394 --> 00:13:06,973
The result was a new argument.

273
00:13:07,103 --> 00:13:09,270
Slavery was not a necessary evil.

274
00:13:09,400 --> 00:13:11,071
Slavery was a positive good.

275
00:13:11,201 --> 00:13:15,433
The master-slave relationship, apologists claimed, resembled a family.

276
00:13:15,563 --> 00:13:18,582
Enslaved people, they said, were happier than the overworked

277
00:13:18,602 --> 00:13:19,915
northern wage slaves.

278
00:13:20,045 --> 00:13:23,195
Slave states tightened their slave codes and moved to prohibit

279
00:13:23,215 --> 00:13:25,561
emancipation of any kind, even voluntary.

280
00:13:25,691 --> 00:13:28,444
Jailings, whippings, and lynchings ended what remained of the

281
00:13:28,464 --> 00:13:29,441
southern debate.

282
00:13:29,571 --> 00:13:32,855
In Congress, southerners pushed through a gag resolution that

283
00:13:32,875 --> 00:13:35,779
tabled every antislavery petition without debate.

284
00:13:35,909 --> 00:13:39,281
In 1835 southern postmasters were ordered to destroy

285
00:13:39,301 --> 00:13:40,725
abolitionist mail.

286
00:13:40,855 --> 00:13:43,531
Free speech itself had become a sectional issue.

287
00:13:43,661 --> 00:13:44,564
And the North?

288
00:13:44,694 --> 00:13:48,747
Most northerners disliked the abolitionists, especially the Garrisonians.

289
00:13:48,877 --> 00:13:51,812
They revered the Constitution's compromises on slavery.

290
00:13:51,942 --> 00:13:55,586
Southern planters owed northern bankers about $300,000,000,

291
00:13:55,606 --> 00:13:58,527
money that would vanish if the Union broke.

292
00:13:58,657 --> 00:14:02,656
But through the 1850s the abolitionist outcry made a dent in the

293
00:14:02,676 --> 00:14:03,805
northern mind.

294
00:14:03,935 --> 00:14:07,644
More and more northerners came to see the South as the land of the unfree.

295
00:14:07,774 --> 00:14:10,313
Every proslavery argument widened the chasm.

296
00:14:10,443 --> 00:14:13,731
Every abolitionist pamphlet widened it from the other side.

297
00:14:13,861 --> 00:14:17,815
Into that gap stepped Abraham Lincoln, who, in the historian

298
00:14:17,835 --> 00:14:21,921
Howard Zinn's words, combined perfectly the needs of business,

299
00:14:21,941 --> 00:14:25,961
the ambition of the new Republican Party, and the rhetoric of

300
00:14:25,981 --> 00:14:27,291
humanitarianism.

301
00:14:27,421 --> 00:14:30,118
He would keep abolition not at the top of his list, but close

302
00:14:30,138 --> 00:14:32,214
enough that pressure could push it there.

303
00:14:32,344 --> 00:14:35,321
Which brings us back to the question the chapter opened with.

304
00:14:35,451 --> 00:14:38,998
Can a nation dedicated to the proposition that all men are

305
00:14:39,018 --> 00:14:43,572
created equal long endure, while 4,000,000 of its people are property?

306
00:14:43,702 --> 00:14:46,180
In 1861, the country would find out.

